            <!-- Section 1 -->
            <div id="why-it-works">
              <div class="section-stamp">🧠 The Why</div>
              <h2>Why a Spin Wheel Works for Weekly Planning</h2>

              <p>
                Planning advice usually assumes the problem is a lack of
                structure — that with a better template or a fancier app, the
                indecision would disappear. In practice, the culprit is usually
                simpler:
                <strong
                  >choosing is tiring, and tired brains choose badly</strong
                >.
              </p>
              <p>
                Researchers describe this as decision fatigue — the tendency for
                judgment to get worse the more choices a person has already made
                that day. By the time you're standing in front of the fridge at
                6 p.m., you've likely made hundreds of smaller decisions
                already, and "what's for dinner" lands on an exhausted brain
                that reaches for the path of least resistance — often takeout,
                or nothing at all.
              </p>

              <div class="callout callout-info">
                <div class="callout-title">📖 Go Deeper</div>
                <p>
                  For more on the science, see our piece on
                  <a
                    href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/psychology-random-decision-making"
                    >the psychology behind random decision making</a
                  >, which covers why outsourcing low-stakes choices to a random
                  tool is a legitimate way to protect mental energy, not a
                  shortcut around effort.
                </p>
              </div>

              <h3>The Rule That Makes the Whole System Work</h3>
              <p>
                <strong
                  >Only put entries on the wheel you'd be genuinely glad to land
                  on.</strong
                >
                This is the entire foundation. A wheel loaded with obligations
                you secretly want to avoid turns into something to be gamed or
                ignored. A wheel loaded only with pre-approved options turns
                into something you trust — because there's no bad outcome
                possible.
              </p>

              <figure class="article-image">
                <img
                  src="/eng-food-picker-wheel.webp"
                  alt="A person sitting at a kitchen table on a Sunday afternoon writing meal and task ideas onto sticky notes before adding them to a spin wheel"
                  loading="lazy"
                  width="1000"
                  height="560"
                />
                <figcaption>
                  Building your wheels once, on a calm Sunday, is what makes the
                  rest of the week effortless.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>

              <div class="callout callout-tip">
                <div class="callout-title">💡 Pro Tip</div>
                <p>
                  Bookmark each finished wheel as its own link. A "Meals Wheel,"
                  a "Workouts Wheel," and a "Tasks Wheel" living in one browser
                  folder means your whole planning system is one tap away, on
                  any device, without ever creating an account.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 2 -->
            <div id="meals">
              <div class="section-stamp">🍽️ Part 1</div>
              <h2>Meal Planning with a Spin Wheel</h2>

              <p>
                "What do you want for dinner?" is one of the most repeated — and
                least enjoyed — questions in any household. Food alone accounts
                for dozens of small decisions a day: what to buy, what to cook,
                what to skip, what to reheat. A dedicated meal wheel collapses
                all of that into a three-second spin.
              </p>
              <p>
                Build it once, ideally on a Sunday when you're not hungry or
                rushed, and trust it for the week that follows.
              </p>

              <h3>Building Your Meal Wheel Step by Step</h3>
              <ul class="step-list" role="list">
                <li>
                  <div class="step-num">1</div>
                  <div class="step-text">
                    <strong>List meals your household actually enjoys.</strong>
                    Resist adding "healthy" options nobody will cook. Aim for
                    10–18 entries — enough range to feel varied, few enough that
                    nothing is forgotten.
                  </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <div class="step-num">2</div>
                  <div class="step-text">
                    <strong>Mix in a couple of wildcards.</strong> Entries like
                    "Order in," "Breakfast for dinner," or "Fridge clear-out
                    night" cover the nights you simply don't have the energy to
                    cook from scratch.
                  </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <div class="step-num">3</div>
                  <div class="step-text">
                    <strong>Spin five times on Sunday.</strong> Those results
                    become Monday through Friday's dinners — decided, done, no
                    renegotiating mid-week.
                  </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <div class="step-num">4</div>
                  <div class="step-text">
                    <strong>Post the results somewhere visible</strong> — a
                    whiteboard, a shared note, a magnet on the fridge — so
                    nobody has to ask "wait, what was Wednesday again?"
                  </div>
                </li>
              </ul>

              <div class="wheel-card">
                <div class="wheel-card-header">
                  <div class="wc-icon">🍽️</div>
                  <h3>Ready-to-Use: Weekly Dinner Wheel</h3>
                  <span class="wc-badge">14 entries · ~15 mins to set up</span>
                </div>
                <div class="wheel-card-body">
                  <p>Paste these straight into the free tool to get started:</p>
                  <div class="wheel-entries">
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Pasta Bolognese</span>
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Stir-fry &amp; Rice</span>
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Grilled Chicken Salad</span>
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Homemade Pizza</span>
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Veggie Curry &amp; Naan</span>
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Taco Night</span>
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Salmon &amp; Greens</span>
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Soup &amp; Crusty Bread</span>
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Shakshuka</span>
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Homemade Burgers</span>
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Roast Chicken</span>
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Takeaway Pick</span>
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Breakfast for Dinner</span>
                    <span class="wheel-entry">Fridge Clear-Out</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="wheel-pro-tip">
                    <strong>💡 Pro tip:</strong> Build a second, smaller wheel
                    with just four or five "low-effort" meals for the nights
                    when a full dinner isn't realistic. Keeping both wheels
                    bookmarked means you always land on something you can
                    actually make.
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>

              <h3>Beyond Dinner: Breakfasts, Lunches &amp; Kids' Meals</h3>
              <p>
                The same approach works for every meal slot. A breakfast wheel
                with six to eight entries (overnight oats, eggs, a yoghurt bowl,
                avocado toast, a smoothie, cereal, or leftovers) removes one
                more decision from the part of the day when willpower is usually
                at its highest — worth protecting.
              </p>
              <p>
                Households with picky eaters often get the most out of a "kids'
                lunch wheel." Letting a child spin it themselves tends to
                increase how willingly they accept the result — a dynamic
                explored further in our guide to
                <a
                  href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/family-game-night-wheel-spinner"
                  >family-friendly wheel activities</a
                >, where shared ownership of the spin turns a potential argument
                into a game everyone wants to play.
              </p>
