            <div id="mistakes">
              <div class="section-stamp">⚠️ Before You Start</div>
              <h2>Mistakes That Undermine a Planning Wheel</h2>
              <p>
                Most people who try this and give up after a week fall into one
                of a few avoidable traps. Watch for these before you build your
                first wheel.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <strong
                    >Adding "should" options instead of "want" options.</strong
                  >
                  A wheel padded with kale salads you never actually eat
                  produces spins you dread. Only add what you'd choose yourself
                  on a good day.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Making one giant wheel for everything.</strong>
                  Mixing meals, tasks, and self-care on a single wheel produces
                  confusing, low-relevance results. Keep each wheel scoped to
                  one category.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Never editing the wheel.</strong> A wheel is a living
                  list, not a one-time setup. Swap in new entries every couple
                  of months, and remove anything that consistently gets skipped.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Treating the result as optional.</strong> The whole
                  benefit disappears if you spin and then second-guess the
                  outcome anyway. If you don't trust yourself to follow through,
                  the entry probably shouldn't be on the wheel.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>

